   192	!! The leaks, named
   193	
   194	* ''The gate can count the vessel keys it has OBSERVED under one face.'' A second vessel presenting under the same `nym` tells that gate at least two vessels wore the face there. It learns no total unless every vessel presents. A gate MUST NOT keep an inventory of a face's current vessels: that inventory would stand as a roster.
   195	* ''A gate that has not learned a withdrawal may still accept.'' No clock repairs that (#/causal-withdrawal), and no proof without a clock, a roster or an online responder bounds it.
   196	* ''A withdrawal history's length tells each gate the face dials how many keys that face has withdrawn there.'' A gate that never met a withdrawn vessel learns the count and nothing of the key.
   197	* ''A session's certificate names its hand's profile'', so a gate tells a spirit's seat from a person's tab. It names no spirit and no Mask.
   198	* ''A replayed first flight draws provisional work'', and tells its sender whether that flight still earns the upgrade. The signed finish still blocks every application effect.
   199	* ''A terminating proxy reads the subprotocol token and the path.'' The household terminator's stock access line keeps the path, and with it the knock, and keeps no line of the token (#/the-browser-envelope). A passive observer of an encrypted connection reads neither the path nor the header.
   200	* ''The knock tests offline'' for an observer holding candidate gate keys: accepted as named, since any pre-filter keyed on public pin material tests the same way (the reading and its measurements stand at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/w28-gate-lane">> #/the-knock-reading).
   201	* ''Address, timing, destination and size stay visible'', as the session ruling names them. Silence at the application layer never amounts to invisibility on the network.
