bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof.mem:32:''Why this stands as its own carrier, beside the session contract.'' The session contract (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract">>) governs the inside of one vessel: its owner, its islands, its holder. This carrier governs the wire: a gate, a herm, a sibling and an observer read it, and none of them reads the holder's protocol. The two meet at one field, the session's proof subject, and each builds on its own order.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof.mem:149:▢ ''OWED'': an operator's contract over their own herm (one signature, one place, one Nexus) names bytes no code holds yet; the arm reads the quorum's `carry` act until it does.
bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor.mem:101:''A BACKUP ASKS THE VESSEL'S ROUTE AGAIN, ON A FRAME OF ITS OWN.'' An unlocked vessel's door answers any process of its own user, and no act of the table hands out a root. A face's backup bundle rebuilds that face's root for whoever holds the bundle's route, so the cut rides a `backup` frame apart from the act table (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-cut">>). The frame carries the vessel's own route, the face's handle-index and the bundle key, and nothing beside them. The holder proves that the route opens the key it already holds before any row opens; a route that opens no slot spends one of the boot's unlock attempts and refuses `no-slot-opens`, and spent attempts refuse `attempts-spent`. The vessel answers the bundle's body sealed under the bundle key, so no row's plaintext leaves it, and the command binds the bundle's routes in its own process, so the vessel never reads them. The door logs how the cut ended, by name, and nothing of the route, the key, the rows or the body.

